Privately Attack Chains =E2=80=94 defensive synthesis

1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS

AC-2026-MCP-OAUTH-ROUTE-HIJACK =E2=80=94 RECENT/LAB_ONLY. Fingerprint:
malicious local dependency -> lifecycle execution -> agent global
config mutation -> persistent session-start hook -> MCP route
substitution -> bearer-token interception -> delegated SaaS authority.
CONFIRMED through token interception in public Mitiga research;
subsequent malicious SaaS actions remain UNKNOWN. Trust boundaries:
npm package -> developer OS -> agent config -> MCP transport -> OAuth
authority. Persistence: session-start reassertion. Detection:
file-integrity changes to agent config, unexpected localhost MCP
endpoints, new lifecycle hooks, outbound token-bearing proxy paths.
Containment: rotate every MCP-connected OAuth grant, reset agent
config/hooks, isolate package execution, minimize scopes. Safe lab:
disposable user profile, dummy OAuth server/token, fake MCP endpoint;
verify config mutation/token interception only.

AC-2026-AGENT-CONFIG-WORM-PERSISTENCE =E2=80=94 RECENT. Fingerprint: poison=
ed
package -> install-time execution -> credential harvesting ->
agent/editor config implantation -> repo push authority -> infected
config committed to accessible repos -> later workspace/session open
-> payload re-execution -> CI/package-publishing authority ->
downstream propagation. Public reporting on the Keyv-linked npm worm
independently documents poisoned packages plus Claude Code/VS Code
hooks and credential harvesting; earlier Mini Shai-Hulud reporting
describes the same agent/editor-config persistence/propagation
mechanism. Exact victim-by-victim downstream execution remains
UNKNOWN. Detection: new .claude/settings.json or .vscode/tasks.json,
SessionStart/runOn-folderOpen changes, package lifecycle scripts,
anomalous repo-wide config commits. Containment: treat agent/editor
config as executable policy, require review/allowlisting, revoke
repo/npm/cloud authority after infection.

2 MATERIAL CHAIN UPDATES

AC-2026-RESIDUAL-AUTHORITY-CASCADE: strengthened by public reporting
on Red Hat npm compromise and prior Checkmarx/Trivy sequence. New
edge: compromised CI/OIDC or surviving credentials -> official package
publication -> downstream CI credential targeting. Red Hat stated no
identified customer/partner or production-system impact, so those
downstream consequence edges remain UNKNOWN rather than inferred.

3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS

A. Developer/package execution -> durable agent/editor configuration
-> later privileged execution.

B. Local compromise -> MCP/OAuth routing -> delegated SaaS authority.

C. CI/release authority -> trusted registry artifact -> downstream
developer/CI credential access.

D. Credential theft -> surviving bot/app/OIDC/publisher authority ->
post-remediation recurrence.

E. Repository-controlled content/config -> agent execution context ->
cross-tool authority.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPHS

MCP OAuth chain: package [CONFIRMED] -> lifecycle execution
[CONFIRMED] -> ~/.claude.json mutation [CONFIRMED] -> SessionStart
persistence [CONFIRMED] -> MCP proxy [CONFIRMED] -> OAuth bearer
interception [CONFIRMED] -> SaaS action [UNKNOWN].

Agent-config worm: poisoned package [CONFIRMED] -> install execution
[CONFIRMED] -> credential harvest [CONFIRMED] -> Claude/VS Code hook
implantation [CONFIRMED] -> repo/package authority [CONFIRMED at
campaign level] -> propagation [CONFIRMED at campaign level] ->
specific downstream privileged action [UNKNOWN].

5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

Whether any public incident documents intercepted MCP OAuth tokens
being used for a confirmed external write/action; whether Keyv-linked
agent-config hooks produced independently verified post-cleanup
re-execution; which identity class enabled the Red Hat CI/OIDC
publication path; whether agent-memory poisoning has crossed from lab
demonstrations to independently documented production incidents.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES

Monitor agent/editor configuration as executable persistence surface;
alert on MCP endpoint changes and localhost proxy substitution;
inventory OAuth grants by agent and tool; isolate package lifecycle
execution from cloud/release credentials; require provenance-bound
publishing identities; revoke human+bot+app+deploy-key+registry+OIDC
authority as a single remediation graph; monitor repository config
propagation separately from source-code diffs.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

(1) Dummy MCP OAuth interception using fake bearer token and local
test server. (2) Disposable repo with benign SessionStart marker to
measure persistence after package removal. (3) Fake CI environment
demonstrating that package lifecycle code cannot access dummy
cloud/release tokens under proposed isolation. No real credentials,
wallets, production repos or third-party systems.

8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS

CL-MUTABLE-AGENT-CONFIG: MCP OAuth hijack + Mini Shai-Hulud +
Keyv-linked hooks. Mechanism: local write authority becomes durable
agent execution/instruction authority.

CL-RELEASE-AUTHORITY-CASCADE: Trivy/Checkmarx + Red Hat + npm worm
cases. Mechanism: developer/CI credential compromise becomes trusted
publishing authority and then downstream execution.

